---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '@/components/PageHero.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '@/components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { POLICY_LAST_UPDATED, SITE } from '@/lib/site';

const updated = new Date(`${POLICY_LAST_UPDATED}T00:00:00Z`).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' });
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Content Accuracy and Disclaimer"
  description="How NeoKemetAI sources its information about ancient Egypt, why interpretations change, and how to read our reconstructions, diagrams and 3D models."
>
  <PageHero kicker="Disclaimer" title="Content accuracy" glyph="𓆄">
    <Breadcrumbs slot="above" items={[{ label: 'Content accuracy', href: '/disclaimer/' }]} />
  </PageHero>

  <div class="container section prose policy">
    <div class="updated">Last updated: <time datetime={POLICY_LAST_UPDATED}>{updated}</time></div>

    <p>
      {SITE.name} is an educational and informational website about ancient Egypt. We aim to base every page on reliable
      archaeological and historical sources, which are listed on the <a href="/about/">about page</a> and, on our most
      detailed pages, next to the information they support.
    </p>

    <h2>Knowledge of the past changes</h2>
    <p>
      Egyptology is an active field. Chronology, the attribution of monuments and objects, the translation of texts and the
      interpretation of sites can all change as excavations, surveys and scientific studies produce new evidence. Dates before
      664 BCE are approximate and differ between published chronologies. Where specialists disagree, we try to say so and to
      describe the main positions rather than present one as settled.
    </p>

    <h2>Reconstructions and visualisations</h2>
    <p>
      Many images, diagrams, 3D models and films on this site are reconstructions. They are built from published
      measurements and descriptions, but they also involve assumptions: about lost parts, colours, surroundings and details
      that no source records. Where our pages mark evidence levels, "documented" or "surveyed" elements follow published data,
      while "approximate", "reconstructed", "interpretation" and "speculative" elements are our best reading of incomplete
      evidence. Visual reconstructions in NeoKemet films are AI-assisted interpretations, not photographs.
    </p>

    <h2>Errors and corrections</h2>
    <p>
      We check our information carefully, but errors, omissions or outdated details can still occur, and we cannot guarantee
      that every page is complete or free of error. If you spot a mistake, please tell us through the
      <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>, ideally with the source that supports the correction.
    </p>

    <h2>Using this site for research</h2>
    <p>
      {SITE.name} is a starting point, not a primary source. For academic work, publication or any decision that depends on
      historical accuracy, please consult the primary sources, museum records, excavation reports and peer-reviewed
      archaeological literature that our pages cite.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .updated { color: var(--text-faint); font-size: var(--step--1); margin-bottom: 1.2rem; }
  .policy h2 { margin-top: 2.2rem; }
</style>
